window.docContents[241].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Interior-regularity":{"contents":"The one-dimensional Poisson theorem directly reconstructs a\n representative and identifies its second weak derivative with\n.\n\nThere is also a raw distributional Poisson result in every dimension. If\n and  against compactly supported\nsmooth tests, then for  and  it constructs\na  representative of . Each second derivative has\n norm at most .\nThe constant depends only on dimension and the fixed smooth bump. No prior\n membership or boundary trace is assumed.\n\nIn arbitrary positive dimension, the compact-set interior theorem starts from\nan  weak solution on an open set ; no zero trace is assumed.\nThe coefficient matrix on  must be the restriction of a globally\nessentially bounded, almost-everywhere uniformly elliptic matrix on\n with globally Lipschitz representatives. With  forcing,\nthis gives an  representative on an open neighborhood of every compact\nsubset of .\n\nThe higher-order local theorem has the same form. Coherent\n coefficient data and  forcing give a\n representative near every compact subset, again without a\nboundary condition. The same global boundedness, ellipticity, and Lipschitz\nhypotheses remain part of this theorem. The higher-order coefficient bundles\nagree almost everywhere with the restricted global field.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Elliptic Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Regularity and geometry","header":"5.7.1.3.1. Interior regularity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Elliptic-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Regularity-and-geometry--Interior-regularity"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Axisymmetric-Flow-with-Swirl/#fluid-axisymmetric-swirl":{"contents":"Axisymmetric flow with swirl is the setting of the computed scenario of\n for three-dimensional Euler blowup. Away\nfrom the axis its equations resemble the planar Boussinesq system, with the\nsquared swirl in the role of the temperature.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics","header":"8.6. Axisymmetric Flow with Swirl","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/Axisymmetric-Flow-with-Swirl/#fluid-axisymmetric-swirl"},"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Verified-symbolic-and-numerical-computation":{"contents":"The heat-mode calculation asks a computer to do something quite specific:\ndifferentiate a product of elementary functions, then recognize that the two\nresulting expressions agree. The weak existence argument asks for something\nelse: quantify over a space of functions and pass to limits. We want the\nfirst calculation to produce a theorem that the second kind of argument can\nuse. That requirement explains both the expression language of the CAS and\nits connection to Mathlib.\n\nThe same division between a candidate calculation and a checked conclusion\nappears in Lewis and Wu's Lean–Mathematica interface\n. Hex supplies verified computational algebra,\nwith Mathlib correspondence layers where available\n. Our task here is to connect calculations to the\nanalytic functions used by a PDE proof: a normalized derivative must agree with\nMathlib's derivative, and an interval must contain the actual real expression.\n\nAn arbitrary function  need not come with a formula\nthat a program can inspect. PDEAtlas therefore represents a limited language\nof formulas as data: variables, constants, arithmetic, powers, trigonometric\nfunctions, exponentials, logarithms, and square roots. A program can walk the\nresulting expression tree, apply the product or chain rule at each node, and\nnormalize the result. A soundness theorem proves that the transformed tree\nhas the claimed meaning as a real function. This is a deep embedding: syntax\nis an object we can compute with, and its interpretation is tied to the\nordinary mathematics by a proof. Users write ordinary Lean notation; the\nconversion to a tree happens internally.\n\nFor the heat mode, the final task is to connect the computed derivatives to\nthe analytic heat residual. The\nresidual bridge theorems supply this\nconnection. Gradient and Laplacian calculations must preserve the coordinate\norder: differentiating in time and differentiating in space are different\noperations even when both coordinates are real numbers. The same distinction\nappears in a smaller example. Treat  as an independent parameter and\ndifferentiate  with respect to :\n\n-- Differentiate in x.\n-- Treat y as an independent parameter.\n#cas [derive x] (sin (x * y))\n\n\nThe factor  is the derivative of the inner expression . The command\nreports:\n\n#cas [derive x]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (sin (x * y))\nOutput (scalar ℝ): cos(x*y)*y\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nA tactic instead closes a stated derivative equality. The next example uses\nMathlib's one-dimensional deriv for the function , rather than the\ntwo-variable inspection calculation above:\n\n-- Discharge an ordinary Mathlib derivative\n-- statement with the tactic.\nexample (x : ℝ) : deriv Real.sin x = Real.cos x := by\n  cas\n\n\nThe inspection command displays a result; the tactic must close the goal\nwith a proof. Exact simplification similarly produces an equality theorem.\nIntegration searches for a supported antiderivative, then differentiates the\ncandidate and checks that its difference from the integrand normalizes to\nzero. Thus finding an antiderivative and accepting it can use different\nprocedures. A complicated search still ends with a derivative calculation\nwhose meaning is proved.\n\nDomain conditions travel with these calculations. The notebook's rational\nidentity cancels , so it assumes . Cancelling \nneeds ; replacing  by  needs . The last\ncondition is stronger than differentiability of Mathlib's real logarithm,\nwhich holds at every nonzero real input. A classical square-root derivative\nrequires a positive argument. Total functions make these distinctions easy\nto miss on the page: an expression still has a value where a proposed rule\nis invalid. The CAS must prove the condition for the particular rule it uses.\nIn the Gaussian calculation, this is why positive time accompanies the\nformula throughout the proof.\n\nThe exact layer includes normalization, differentiation, selected\nantiderivative search, polynomial reasoning, and real-root certificates.\nIts expressions form a fixed grammar of real elementary functions, and its\nintegrator searches a finite family of antiderivative forms. A goal outside\nthe grammar, or one that the search cannot settle, is left unchanged and the\ntactic reports an error.\n\nFor explicit expressions, the checked tactics let the proof concentrate on\nhow a calculation is used. Abstract functions, symbolic dimension, norms and\ninner products, sums, piecewise formulas, and vector-valued derivatives can\nstill require direct Mathlib arguments. This division is useful in the\nGaussian example: a concrete coordinate residual is a good CAS calculation,\nwhile normalization and convergence to the initial datum require analysis.\nLike Mathlib's reflected algebra and arithmetic tactics, the CAS proves\nstatements about Mathlib's own objects. Its additional expression and\ncertificate machinery handles the continuous operations needed here.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.5. Verified symbolic and numerical computation","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Verified-symbolic-and-numerical-computation"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction":{"contents":"Finite-dimensional trial spaces turn the weak PDE into a system of\nCarathéodory ODEs with a pivot mass operator. Stability of the projections in\nboth  and , together with density, lets their solutions approximate the\ninfinite-dimensional problem.\n\nThe construction has four distinct stages:\n\n1. restrict the form and load to each trial space and solve the finite system;2. derive energy bounds independent of the trial dimension;3. extract a weak state limit and pass to the integrated identity;4. use the energy argument to identify the unique limit.\n\nThe quantitative time-Sobolev estimate turns Lions–Magenes regularity into a\nbound on the continuous representative; the parabolic regularity theorems and\nthe weak wave theory use it to control time-dependent forcing and\ndifferentiated Galerkin families. When the energy-to-pivot inclusion is\ncompact, the Aubin–Lions theorem combines that time regularity with the\nspectral Galerkin tail estimate to produce strong compactness in\n.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation","header":"6.3.1.4. Galerkin Construction","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Weak-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Galerkin-Construction"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation--Cole___Hopf___-a-nonlinear-to-linear-transformation--Why-the-Cole___Hopf-transform-works":{"contents":"The Cole–Hopf transform (Cole, 1951; Hopf, 1950; Evans, 2010, pp. 206–207)\nsends a nonvanishing heat solution  to the solution  of\nviscous Burgers.\n\nFor the converse calculation, suppose  and  is a sufficiently\nsmooth Burgers solution on . The logarithmic derivative\ndetermines  only up to a time-dependent factor:\n\n\n\nChoosing a nonzero  satisfying this scalar equation makes  solve\nthe heat equation. Fixing  does not work in general: for \nand  it gives , whereas  is a heat solution with\nthe same Cole–Hopf quotient. We formalize the forward heat-to-Burgers\nimplication.\n\nFor that implication, suppose  is  and solves .\nDefine . Then:\n\n1. First derivative. Differentiating the quotient:\n.2. Second derivative. Differentiating again in space:\n.3. Time derivative. From  and :\n.4. Assembly. The nonlinear term  combines with  and :\nthe common-denominator numerator cancels term by term, leaving\n.\n\nThe formal proof has two layers: a jet-level polynomial cancellation that\nholds algebraically, and an analytic domain theorem that supplies the\nregularity (quotient rules, mixed-derivative commutation, nonvanishing of\n) needed to justify each step.\n\nFor a heat profile , the Cole–Hopf transform isDivision in Lean is total, so the theorems about the transform assume\nseparately that  is nonzero on the domain under consideration.\n\nThe cancellation that makes the transform work is purely algebraic and can be\nisolated first: treat\n at one point as six real numbers\n, impose  and , and check that the\nquotient-rule expressions for , , and  satisfy the\nBurgers identity.\n\nThe algebraic jet identity isolates the exact cancellation: the heat equation\nand its spatial derivative imply that the quotient-rule jets of the\nCole–Hopf transform satisfy\n\nDifferentiate  formally in  and twice in , put all\nterms over a common power of , and substitute  together\nwith . The numerator then cancels term by term,\nleaving zero. No analytic hypothesis enters at this level.\n\nTurning the jet identity into a theorem about functions requires that the\nsix jet values are the value and derivatives of , and that the quotient-rule\nexpressions give the derivatives of . That is where\n regularity, nonvanishing of , and openness of the domain enter,\nsince the quotient rule and the commutation  are local\nstatements.\n\nOn an arbitrary open spacetime domain, if , , andthen  satisfies\n\nThe analytic hypotheses justify the quotient rules and commutation of mixed\npartials. Differentiating the heat equation supplies the spatial derivative\nidentity, after which the jet-level polynomial and rational cancellation\ncloses the viscous Burgers residual.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Cole–Hopf Transformation\u0009Cole–Hopf: a nonlinear-to-linear transformation","header":"9.2.1.5.1.1. Why the Cole–Hopf transform works","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation--Cole___Hopf___-a-nonlinear-to-linear-transformation--Why-the-Cole___Hopf-transform-works"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-whole-space-Hilbert-scale":{"contents":"For , the order- Bessel symbol isThe implementation is complex-valued because the Fourier theory of tempered\ndistributions is complex-linear, although  itself is a positive\nreal number.  The identitiesare the algebraic facts behind the nested scale and the preservation of\nreal-valuedness.\n\nThe symbol determines the space. A tempered distribution belongs to \nexactly when  times its Fourier transform is square integrable, and the\n function so obtained, transported back to physical space, is the\nrepresentative on which the Hilbert structure is built.\n\nFor every real order , the complex Bessel-potential space isWe use Mathlib's BesselPotentialSpace directly, with its notation\nH^{s}(ℝ^n). Mathlib supplies the norm, Hilbert structure, and completeness;\nour derivative and domain results are built on this space.The representative toLp u is characterized byas an identity of tempered distributions.  Applying the inverse Bessel\npotential constructs BesselPotentialSpace.ofLp, and the two maps form the linear isometric\nequivalence BesselPotentialSpace.toLpₗᵢ.  Thus every  is complete and abstractly\nisometric to , while the embedding of that abstract Hilbert space into\ntempered distributions still depends essentially on .\n\nBecause the inner product is transported along toLp, the norm identity that\nfollows is a definition rather than an estimate, and every constant appearing\nlater in the chapter is measured against it.\n\nThe Sobolev norm is exactly the norm of the Bessel representative:\n\nMathlib transports the inner product from  along toLp.\nInjectivity follows by applying the inverse Bessel potential.  The norm\nidentity is therefore immediate from the transported inner product structure.\n\nThe Bessel representative lives in physical space. Multiplier estimates are\nmore transparent on the frequency side, so we use the  Fourier transform\n𝓕 (toLp u).\n\nThe Fourier representative isin .  This identity is understood through\nthe canonical embedding of  into tempered distributions.  Plancherel\ncomposed with BesselPotentialSpace.toLpₗᵢ gives another linear isometric equivalence\n\nPlancherel is an isometry of , so passing from the Bessel representative\nto the Fourier representative costs nothing in norm, and the defining norm can\nequally be computed on the frequency side.\n\nPlancherel gives the exact Fourier-side norm formula\n\nApply the Bessel norm identity and then the  Plancherel isometry to\ntoLp u.\n\nThe first consequence of the frequency-side formula is the nesting of the\nscale. Lowering the order from  to  multiplies the Fourier\nrepresentative by , and the symbol identity \nrecorded with the definition of the symbol is what makes this multiplication\nact as the identity on underlying distributions.\n\nIf , then the identity on underlying tempered distributions defines\na continuous inclusionOn Fourier representatives this lowers the order by multiplication with\n.  Since , that multiplier has pointwise norm at most\none.\n\nFactor the target weight as .  The symbol  is an\n multiplier of norm at most one, so multiplication by it is a\ncontraction on .  Transfer the estimate back through the Fourier\nisometries.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.1. The whole-space Hilbert scale","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--The-whole-space-Hilbert-scale"}});